						<td width="100%" class="body" valign="top">
														I played with chat channels last night and they do work.<br />
<br />
Here are the basics, the full blown details are in the eqmanual_supplement.doc in your everquest directory.<br />
<br />
You can join up to 10 channels.  There are 2 ways of doing this.<br />
<br />
/chat set &lt;channel-list&gt;<br />
and<br />
/join &lt;channel-list&gt;<br />
<br />
the difference is using /chat set removes you from all previously joined channels.<br />
<br />
example:<br />
/chat set raid1:oon, raid2, raid3<br />
or<br />
/join raid1:oon, raid2, raid3<br />
<br />
Both examples join you to 3 chat channels - using chat set these would be channels /1 /2 and /3.  With /join you need to look at the join message to see the assigned channel.  Notice raid1:oon is password protected - the &quot;:oon&quot; is the password.  To join a protected channel you must have the password.<br />
<br />
2 advantages to passworded channels.  Obviously private - and they are the only semipermanent channels.  They remain until 24 hour of being empty.<br />
<br />
To talk is simple:<br />
/1 hey guys - this would broadcast to all listeners on raid1.<br />
<br />
To leave a channel - /leave raid1 or /leave 1<br />
To leave all channels - /leaveall<br />
<br />
/list will display all channels you are on<br />
/list 1 will display all members on raid1<br />
<br />
/announce will toggle announcements on and off.  With it turned on, anytime someone joins a channel you are notified.<br />
<br />
To join a channel automatically when you log on.<br />
/autojoin raid1:oon<br />
<br />
Everytime I log on I would automatically join the raid1 channel, as long as it is still active. Since it is password protected, there is a good chance it will be active still.<br />
<br />
These are the basics - I have not covered moderator.  When you try to join a channel that does not exist, you become the moderator of a new channel.						</td>
